The de Broglie wavelength \(\lambda\) is given by \(\lambda = \frac{h}{p}\), where \(p\) is the momentum. The kinetic energy \(K\) is related to momentum by \(K = \frac{p^2}{2m}\), so \(p = \sqrt{2mK}\). For a gas molecule, the average translational kinetic energy is given by \(K = \frac{3}{2}kT\).
\lambda = \frac{h}{\sqrt{2mK}} = \frac{h}{\sqrt{2m\left(\frac{3}{2}kT\right)}}
\lambda = \frac{h}{\sqrt{3mkT}}
Given values are:
• Planck's constant, \(h = 6.63 \times 10^{-34}\) J.s
• Boltzmann constant, \(k = 1.38 \times 10^{-23}\) J/K
• Mass of oxygen molecule, \(m = 5.31 \times 10^{-26}\) kg
• Temperature, \(T = 27^{\circ}C = 27 + 273 = 300\) K
Substituting these values into the equation:
\lambda = \frac{6.63 \times 10^{-34}}{\sqrt{3 \times 5.31 \times 10^{-26} \times 1.38 \times 10^{-23} \times 300}}
\lambda = \frac{6.63 \times 10^{-34}}{\sqrt{6592.86 \times 10^{-49}}} = \frac{6.63 \times 10^{-34}}{\sqrt{65.9286 \times 10^{-48}}}
\lambda = \frac{6.63 \times 10^{-34}}{8.12 \times 10^{-24}} \approx 0.816 \times 10^{-10} \text{ m}
